<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Assessment Types and Resources</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02548</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Assessment Types and Resources</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0563d7ea-a0d3-41da-9757-07616f66ce80</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Types of Assessments </title>
      <para id="para-00001">As students complete assignments in this course, they encounter
        different types of assessment tasks. The questions that populate these assessments address
        varying levels of rigor as identified by <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f5c7b75ca0ba28e85ee262453e3196a3c618d163">Costa’s
        levels of questioning:</link></para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>Gathering (level 1) - questions that focus on recall and basic procedures</item>
        <item>Processing (level 2) - questions that require combining facts, discerning patterns,
          and sense-making</item>
        <item>Applying (level 3) - questions that invite abstract thinking and synthesizing
          information from multiple sources</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00002">Whether the assessment item assesses the full rigor level of the TEKS or
        only a portion of the TEKS depends upon the type of the assessment and its location within
        the learning progression for each lesson or unit</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Diagnostic Assessments </title>
      <para id="para-00003">Upon first entering a unit, students and teachers have an opportunity to
        assess student understanding of the prerequisite content needed for success in the unit:</para>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <!-- Accordion Item 1-->
          <title>Overview and Readiness Questions</title>

            <para id="para-00004">The <emphasis>Overview
        and Readiness section</emphasis> within each unit contains a three-item assessment aligned
        to prerequisite skills students should have mastered in prior grade levels or previous
        units. </para>
            <list id="list-00002">
          <item>If a student incorrectly answers one of the questions in the assessment, they are
        automatically pushed to a mini-lesson and additional practice opportunities covering the
        topic for that item. </item>
          <item>If a student answers an item correctly, they move on to the next item or to the end
        of the overview and readiness section if that is the last item of the assessment. </item>
        </list>
            <para id="para-00005">Remember, if you wish to direct students to a specific mini-lesson topic,
        have students use the right-navigation menu for the lesson.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">The
        following image illustrates a question from the Unit 2 Overview and Readiness Quiz. Notice
        the right-navigation Lesson menu that students can use to move between the pages and access
        the mini-lessons.</para>
                  <media alt="A screenshot from the curriculum showing an example assessment item that measures student understanding of prerequisite skills. To the right of the multiple choice question, the image includes the navigation menu that can be used to access mini-lessons about the prerequisite skills.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_12_02_03_01.png"/>
        </media>
            
            <para id="para-00007">Also, recall that additional information regarding the vertical alignment
        of TEKS for each unit is provided within each unit level teacher guide. The Unit Resources
        section on this guide contains further suggestions for supplementary content that may be
        useful in supporting student gaps identified by the Overview and Readiness diagnostic tool
        or your own observations.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The following image depicts the
        section of the Unit Resources available on the Unit Teacher Guide.</para>
                  <media alt="A screenshot from the curriculum showing a section of the teacher overview page titled Unit Resources. The items listed include &quot;Vertical Alignment for Unit 2,&quot; &quot;Pre-Algebra Resources from OpenStax,&quot; and &quot;Assessments.&quot;">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_12_02_03_02.png"/>
        </media>
            
            <para id="para-00009">The prerequisite items in the Overview and Readiness section have been
        written to check student understanding and mastery of prior topics. So, while they measure
        student proficiency at a mastery level, the content comes from material students should
        already be familiar with and know. These items are all multiple choice, so they can
        automatically trigger a student pathway towards mini-lesson refreshers if the student
        answers incorrectly.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">
          <emphasis effect="italics">Focused on gathering and processing, these items assess student
        mastery at the full rigor level of the TEKS from previous grade levels or units.</emphasis>
        </para></note>

      <para id="para-00011">The goal for these unit-level diagnostic assessments is to help both
        students and teachers pre-assess topics and skills that students need to successfully
        complete the unit. Information from this assessment can be used to identify needed topics
        for in-lesson scaffolds, small group instruction, tutoring, etc.</para>
      <para id="para-00012">An additional set of assessments intended to provide teachers with
        information at the lesson level is also available. The purpose of these instruments is to
        more formally help teachers monitor student progress and identify student preparedness for
        the lesson content using a variety of question types and item styles.</para>
      <para id="para-00013">These materials, when encountered before the denoted lesson, support
        access to the lesson and identify potential areas where additional support may be required.</para>

        <table class="unnumbered" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="2">
          
